# Dart / Flutter build output and tooling state.
#
# A build directory must never be committed: it is machine-specific, it is
# regenerated by every `flutter build`, and it is large enough to make a diff
# unreviewable. `.build/` is here alongside `build/` because the Swift SDK repo
# (SwiftPM) uses that spelling, and the three SDK repos share this file's rules.
.dart_tool/
.packages
.pub-cache/
.pub/
build/
.build/
coverage/
doc/api/
.flutter-plugins
.flutter-plugins-dependencies

# Native build output from the plugins this SDK depends on. The example app's
# platform folders are ignored wholesale below, but these patterns are
# unanchored so they also catch a hand-made scratch project anywhere in the
# tree.
.gradle/
Pods/
*.xcworkspace/xcuserdata/
*.xcodeproj/xcuserdata/
DerivedData/
.cxx/
local.properties

# The published package's own `pubspec.lock` is deliberately NOT committed.
# This repo publishes a library, not an application: a committed lock file
# would pin resolutions that consumers never use, and it would go stale against
# the version ranges in pubspec.yaml.
#
# `example/pubspec.lock` is committed, and the distinction is the point. The
# example is an application, and it is what CI builds. Leaving it unpinned
# meant every CI run re-resolved every plugin, so a green run pinned nothing
# and an upstream publish could turn this repo red with no change to it — which
# is exactly how the Kotlin 2.x breakage arrived. It also contradicted this
# workflow's own reason for pinning FLUTTER_VERSION: "a floating channel means
# a Flutter release can turn a green PR red with no change to this repo".
#
# Anchored with a leading slash so it means "the one at the repo root" and not
# "every pubspec.lock anywhere", which is what silently swallowed the example's.
/pubspec.lock

# Generated platform folders in the example app. `flutter create .` regenerates
# them from `example/pubspec.yaml`. The four below stay ignored because nothing
# in this repository builds them: the SDK supports Android and iOS, so those
# folders exist only because `flutter create` writes every platform it knows,
# and a folder nobody builds rots without anyone noticing.
#
# `example/android/` and `example/ios/` are tracked, and it is one argument
# applied twice. Building the example is the only step that proves the SDK's
# plugins — `sqflite_sqlcipher`, `flutter_secure_storage`, `url_launcher` —
# actually link on a real target; no unit test reaches that. While these were
# regenerated, that step built a project nobody had ever reviewed and silently
# discarded the hand-edits the app needs: the `<queries>` element on Android,
# `LSApplicationQueriesSchemes` on iOS, the launcher icon, the display name.
# Silently is the word that matters — an iOS example missing that array builds
# and runs perfectly while reporting "not installed" for a Tracive app that is.
# `doc/oauth.md` still carries both, because every integrator must add them to
# their own app; that makes it documentation, not this repo's restore procedure.
#
# The cost is real and worth naming: a Flutter upgrade now lands as an Xcode
# project diff as well as a Gradle one. That is exactly the diff a maintainer
# should be reading.
#
# Both are tracked minus what the Flutter tool writes for itself — generated
# xcconfigs, plugin registrants, `Pods/`, `.symlinks/`, every `xcuserdata` —
# see `example/android/.gitignore` and `example/ios/.gitignore`.
example/linux/
example/macos/
example/windows/
example/web/
example/.flutter-plugins
example/.flutter-plugins-dependencies

# IDE and editor state.
.idea/
*.iml
*.iws
.vscode/
*.swp
.history/

# macOS.
.DS_Store

# Local overrides. `pubspec_overrides.yaml` is how a developer points this
# package at a local checkout of a dependency; that is a machine-local choice.
pubspec_overrides.yaml
.env
*.log
